/*Q23. A company wants to calculate an employee's revised salary after adding a bonus. However, the original salary stored in the payroll system
must remain unchanged.
Write a Java program using the EmployeeBonus class to demonstrate pass-by-value for primitive data types.
Implement the following static method:
calculateBonus(int salary): Accepts the employee's salary as an int parameter, adds 5000 to the parameter, and prints the updated salary
using the format Updated "Salary: <updated_salary>".
Class: EmployeeBonus
The EmployeeBonus class should:
1. Read the employee's original salary as an integer.
2. Call calculateBonus(int salary) with the entered salary.
35 Print the original salary after the method call using the format "Original Salary: <original_salary>".
Input Format
• The input consists of a single integer representing the employee's salary.
Output Format
• Print the output in the following format:
Updated Salary: <updated_salary>
Original Salary: <original_salary>
*/
import java.util.Scanner;
 
public class EmployeeBonus {
 
    static void calculateBonus(int salary) {
        salary = salary + 5000;              // changes only the local copy
        System.out.println("Updated Salary: " + salary);
    }
 
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int salary = sc.nextInt();
        calculateBonus(salary);
        System.out.println("Original Salary: " + salary);
        sc.close();
    }
}
